`Tally` is a **struct** that implements an interface `ICounter`:

```csharp
public interface ICounter { void Add(); }

public struct Tally : ICounter
{
    public int Count;
    public void Add() => Count++;
}
```

This code runs:

```csharp
var t = new Tally();      // Count = 0
ICounter boxed = t;
boxed.Add();
t.Add();
t.Add();
ICounter alias = boxed;
alias.Add();

Console.WriteLine(t.Count);
Console.WriteLine(((Tally)boxed).Count);
```

The first line printed is {{local}}.

The second line printed is {{boxed}}.

`Line` is a **struct** with a mutating method, and `Order` is a class that exposes a `Line` through an auto-property. This program runs:

```csharp
var order = new Order
{
    Line = new Line { Qty = 1, Tags = new List<string>() }
};

order.Line.Bump();
Console.WriteLine($"{order.Line.Qty} {order.Line.Tags.Count}");

public struct Line
{
    public int Qty;
    public List<string> Tags;
    public void Bump()
    {
        Qty++;
        Tags.Add("bumped");
    }
}

public sealed class Order
{
    public Line Line { get; set; }
}
```

What does the `Console.WriteLine` call print?
